+Open a workbench: a directory in the journal for working material that outlives a session
+
+`arc journal workbench <slug> --purpose TEXT` creates `workbench/<slug>-<YYYYMMDD>/` under the journal with an index file, `WORKBENCH.md`, records a `workbench-opened` event, and prints the directory's absolute path as its last line. Anything may be written inside it. Arc records opening, adopting, linking, closing and archiving, never the content, and reads the content only to count files and bytes and to see when anything in it last changed. It rewrites the index from those records at each one.
+
+Opening a slug already open with the same purpose records nothing and prints that workbench's path; another purpose refuses. A second workbench of one slug and day takes a numeric suffix. `catchup` and `journal open` list open workbenches with purpose, size and age, and mark one stale once neither a record nor a file in it has changed for 14 days. `journal archive <name>` moves a closed workbench to the cold archive and `journal unarchive <name>` brings it back. The subcommands below name a workbench by its name, or by its slug while one workbench of that slug is open. Journal exchange carries no workbench: `arc guide journal-workbenches`
+
+Usage: arc journal workbench [OPTIONS] --purpose <PURPOSE> <SLUG>
+       arc journal workbench <COMMAND>
+
+Commands:
+  list   List open workbenches with purpose, size and age (arc-journal-workbenches/1)
+  link   Link a workbench to a change or to an artifact of this journal
+  close  Close a workbench, naming what it produced
