+===== arc journal workbench adopt --help =====
+Adopt an existing directory or file as a workbench, moving it into the journal
+
+A directory is renamed into `workbench/` whole; a file is moved into a workbench directory of its own. A rename keeps every byte, mode and time of the material, and arc adds only the index. The workbench is named `<slug>-<YYYYMMDD>`: the slug defaults to the path's name without its extension, and a name ending in `-YYYYMMDD` keeps that date. Records a `workbench-adopted` event with where the material was, its files and its bytes, and prints the new path as the last line.
+
+The material is opened, without following a link, once the journal's transition lock is held, and every check reads that opened object. Every refusal is decided before anything moves, and leaves the material as it was. By what the path names, adoption refuses a path that does not exist; a symbolic link, with or without a trailing slash; anything but a file or a directory; a filesystem root or a mount point; a file named `WORKBENCH.md`; and a path its record cannot state on one line of text.
+
+By where it lies: the current directory, the home directory, or a directory holding either; anything inside a `.git` or `.arc` directory; a path on another filesystem than the journal, which no rename can move atomically; a path a Git repository tracks, its name read literally; anything inside a Git repository's own directory or inside a journal; and a path that holds, or lies inside, storage arc keeps: this journal's own records, artifacts and archive, another journal, or arc's home.
+
+By what it holds: a Git repository, bare or not; a journal, registered or not, known by its bindings, its locks, or an event log beside artifacts; a mount point; a relative symbolic link leading out of it, which would point elsewhere once moved; and a `WORKBENCH.md` at its top that arc did not write. A slug already open refuses too.
+
+The move is one rename that replaces nothing, made only while the material's path still leads to the object checked; material changed after its checks refuses, and a kernel or filesystem with no such rename refuses the move. A system without `/dev/fd` or `/proc/self/fd`, through which arc reads and names the directories it opens, refuses before anything is read. `arc guide journal-workbenches` says what follows when the move is made and its record is not.
+
+A directory of the journal arc does not own, such as an entry of `loose/`, may be adopted. A directory under `workbench/` that no event records is adopted where it stands
+
+Usage: arc journal workbench adopt [OPTIONS] --purpose <PURPOSE> <PATH>
+
+Arguments:
+  <PATH>
+          The directory or file to move into the journal
+
+Options:
+      --actor <ACTOR>
+          Acting identity; see `arc guide identity-options`
